<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shinto</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shinto is the indigenous religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, centred on the worship of kami, spirits and deities present in nature, ancestors and places, at shrines served by priests. It was intertwined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> for over a thousand years until the Meiji government separated them in 1868 and promoted State Shinto with the emperor at its centre. Under Japanese colonial rule, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> and Taiwanese were made to worship at Shinto shrines.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shinto.jpg" alt="Shinto" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JordyMeow · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Itsukushima_Gate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
